 # Shallow MLP Decoder
 
-Also called: Color Decoder, Decoder Architectures, Density Decoder, Implicit MLP, Volume Density and Color Decoder
+Also called: Color Decoder, Decoder Architectures, Density Decoder, Density and Color Decoders, Implicit MLP, Volume Density and Color Decoder
 
 ## Overview
-The Shallow MLP Decoder is a neural network component that transforms a concatenated feature embedding vector into volume density and per-point color values [S1, unofficial]. It serves as the final stage in explicit neural feature volume pipelines, acting as a regularizer to improve reconstruction quality in difficult conditions [S1, 2301-decoder-network.md, unofficial]. It is distinguished from deeper implicit MLPs (like standard NeRF) by its shallow depth and its reliance on pre-computed feature embeddings rather than raw coordinates, offering a trade-off between performance and resource utilization [S1, unofficial].
+The Shallow MLP Decoder is a neural network component that transforms a concatenated feature embedding vector into volume density and per-point color values [S2, unofficial]. It serves as the final stage in explicit neural feature volume pipelines, acting as a regularizer to improve reconstruction quality in difficult conditions [S1, 2301-decoder-network.md]. It is distinguished from deeper implicit MLPs by its shallow depth and reliance on pre-computed feature embeddings rather than raw coordinates, offering a trade-off between performance and resource utilization [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The decoder takes a feature vector $F$ as input and outputs a scalar density $\sigma$ and a color vector $c$ [S1, unofficial].
+The decoder takes a feature vector $F$ as input and outputs a scalar density $\sigma$ and a color vector $c$ [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** Feature vector $F$ of size $L \cdot F$, where $L$ is the number of resolution levels and $F$ is the feature dimension per level [S1, unofficial]. For example, with 16 levels and 4 dimensions, $F$ has size 64 [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Output:** Scalar density $\sigma$ and vector color $c$ (typically 3 channels) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** Feature vector $F$ of size $L \cdot F$, where $L$ is the number of resolution levels and $F$ is the feature dimension per level [S2, unofficial]. For example, with 16 levels and 4 dimensions, $F$ has size 64 [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** Scalar density $\sigma$ and vector color $c$ (typically 3 channels) [S2, unofficial].
 
